WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.280
So what I'd like you to do is to actually give this a shot,

00:04.280 --> 00:08.320
practice around using the idea of a composable in your directory.

00:08.320 --> 00:11.000
So basically, I kind of wanted to outline it a bit.

00:11.000 --> 00:13.760
You'll create a Composables folder inside of your source directory.

00:13.760 --> 00:14.860
Look inside of your app,

00:14.860 --> 00:18.540
see what opportunities there might be to share some state or functionality.

00:18.540 --> 00:20.440
Or if you don't have it obviously,

00:20.440 --> 00:23.380
like I did earlier, just go and do so to practice it,

00:23.380 --> 00:25.160
practice sharing those things.

00:25.160 --> 00:28.720
So here, I said create a file called useState or useFunction.

00:28.720 --> 00:30.360
Basically, I know in my case,

00:30.360 --> 00:33.280
I call it countStore which is a lead-in for something else in the future.

00:33.280 --> 00:34.640
But generally speaking,

00:34.640 --> 00:36.240
when you're naming your Composables,

00:36.240 --> 00:37.880
you do prefix it with that use.

00:37.880 --> 00:41.400
So in that case, I should have probably named it like useCountStore for example.

00:41.400 --> 00:44.920
Then otherwise, just try stuff out.

00:46.800 --> 00:50.760
All right. So how that feel with the Composables?

00:50.760 --> 00:55.200
Is that a bit weird to do?

00:55.200 --> 00:56.720
I see a lot of people still typing,

00:56.720 --> 00:58.600
so probably so tinkering away.

00:58.600 --> 01:00.080
People in the chat,

01:00.080 --> 01:03.880
if you had any issues with those Composables, please let me know.

01:03.880 --> 01:06.760
So I'm just going ahead and walk through a simple example for

01:06.760 --> 01:08.280
the solution and talk through

01:08.280 --> 01:10.840
some theoretical things while we're at it.

01:10.840 --> 01:14.640
So let's go ahead and switch over to sayLoveView.

01:14.640 --> 01:18.820
So if we look at sayLoveView, what do we have?

01:18.820 --> 01:20.880
Well, we have a couple things going on.

01:20.880 --> 01:22.080
We have a user card here,

01:22.080 --> 01:23.480
that's fine, user page.

01:23.480 --> 01:26.800
Okay. We have some async stuff happening.

01:26.800 --> 01:28.560
All right. Well, this is in the script setup.

01:28.560 --> 01:30.520
So this actually might be a good one.

01:30.520 --> 01:33.640
So let's say for instance then,

01:33.640 --> 01:37.400
that we want the ability to display the users in more than one place.

01:37.400 --> 01:39.040
Because the user page is a great place to list it,

01:39.040 --> 01:40.360
but on the other hand,

01:40.360 --> 01:42.960
there might be reasons to actually display it on like the homepage for instance.

01:42.960 --> 01:46.440
So let's just abstract that away into a Composable.

01:46.440 --> 01:51.240
So I'm going to go ahead and create a new folder here for Composables.

01:51.240 --> 01:54.480
Then I'm going to create one and following the syntax now,

01:54.480 --> 01:57.920
I'm going to call it useUserStore.js.

01:57.920 --> 02:00.560
All we're going to do here is we're going to do

02:00.560 --> 02:05.440
an import reactive from view.

02:05.440 --> 02:13.120
Then we're going to export a const called userList.

02:13.120 --> 02:14.920
Although actually, you know what I'm going to do?

02:14.920 --> 02:16.560
I'm actually going to do,

02:16.560 --> 02:19.920
because we're actually just exporting the single array,

02:19.920 --> 02:22.520
I prefer usually to use ref when it's not an object.

02:22.520 --> 02:25.160
So reactive is usually when I think of like a more complex object,

02:25.160 --> 02:27.400
that's personally kind of where I lean towards.

02:27.400 --> 02:29.840
So userList is a ref of an array.

02:29.840 --> 02:31.320
That's all this is.

02:31.320 --> 02:33.200
So now that we have this here,

02:33.200 --> 02:37.000
we can go inside of user page and we can change this up basically to say,

02:37.000 --> 02:43.220
okay, so I'm going to import from what?

02:43.220 --> 02:45.800
We're going to go up one level,

02:45.800 --> 02:49.440
go to Composables, go to useUserStore,

02:49.440 --> 02:53.200
and then what we're looking for inside of here is the userList,

02:53.200 --> 02:54.960
which is autocompleted right there.

02:54.960 --> 02:58.000
So now we can technically delete this,

02:58.000 --> 03:01.480
and then this means we can delete the reactive up here,

03:01.480 --> 03:03.880
and then wherever userList is being called,

03:03.880 --> 03:05.280
we will remove the state,

03:05.280 --> 03:07.360
and then because we're in script setup,

03:07.360 --> 03:08.760
remember that it's a ref,

03:08.760 --> 03:10.840
so we need to unpack it with dot value.

03:10.840 --> 03:13.040
Again, it's nice because the tooling has updated now,

03:13.040 --> 03:16.480
so that it'll actually tell you that it's expected the dot value.

03:16.480 --> 03:18.520
There was a time where the tooling hadn't caught up,

03:18.520 --> 03:21.880
and so it was really easy to miss that on your own if you weren't paying attention.

03:21.880 --> 03:24.560
So there we go, userList is now available.

03:24.560 --> 03:27.560
We can save and everything should still look good.

03:27.560 --> 03:28.680
Yeah, it looks good to me.

03:28.680 --> 03:29.880
If we go to Home now,

03:29.880 --> 03:32.560
let's actually show that we can do something similar.

03:32.560 --> 03:36.400
So we'll go now over to the Home page.

03:36.400 --> 03:41.400
So Home page, and I'll just use the script setup again up here,

03:41.400 --> 03:46.280
and let me just actually copy that snippet over from up here.

03:46.360 --> 03:50.360
So import, there we go, userList from that.

03:50.360 --> 03:52.200
Import userList right here,

03:52.200 --> 03:54.280
and again, once we've imported it,

03:54.280 --> 03:58.760
the mental model shift that you have to think about here is that it is already available.

03:58.760 --> 04:03.960
So we can go ahead and just do an LI in this case,

04:03.960 --> 04:11.360
and then v4 userInUserList and then user.name.

04:11.360 --> 04:12.680
I think that should work.

04:12.680 --> 04:16.120
There we go. It's looking good.

04:16.120 --> 04:20.800
Then to show you why this is incredibly powerful is that maybe in this specific context,

04:20.800 --> 04:23.640
this is where I want to actually only show the first five.

04:23.640 --> 04:26.240
So I don't need this to pollute the entire file.

04:26.240 --> 04:32.760
What I can do is I can just import the computer property from view,

04:32.760 --> 04:42.960
and just say const short userList is a computer property that

04:43.000 --> 04:48.640
returns userList.value,

04:48.640 --> 04:56.520
what was it, splice05, I think.

04:56.520 --> 04:58.080
I think that returns it. Great.

04:58.080 --> 05:01.200
Then we can do short userList here.

05:01.200 --> 05:03.120
You'll notice, there we go.

05:03.120 --> 05:06.520
Now we have this global piece of data that is shared in two files,

05:06.520 --> 05:09.840
but we also have some very specific context on how we might want to manipulate

05:09.840 --> 05:11.840
that data for that one component.

05:11.840 --> 05:13.640
That in it of itself is, I think,

05:13.640 --> 05:17.600
the power of the idea of the composition API in that you can really start to

05:17.600 --> 05:20.320
compose things the way that makes sense to you.

05:20.320 --> 05:21.920
So the other thing about View3,

05:21.920 --> 05:23.000
which you're not going to cover at all,

05:23.000 --> 05:25.760
but I did want to make a mention to it because those coming from View2 or

05:25.760 --> 05:28.000
those looking at View3 docs might notice that

05:28.000 --> 05:32.360
Mixins are a thing that also were particularly popular during the View2 days,

05:32.360 --> 05:34.120
and Mixins what they basically allow you to do,

05:34.120 --> 05:38.280
it was a way for you to share functionality and state using the options API.

05:38.280 --> 05:40.360
Nowadays, I would say the fact that you have these

05:40.360 --> 05:44.280
composables and reusable pieces of state and function that's easily

05:44.280 --> 05:46.680
injectable in a very concise way,

05:46.680 --> 05:51.240
these are basically superior to Mixins in every way, shape, and form.

05:51.240 --> 05:55.440
It's really actually hard to argue for using Mixins over the composables.

05:55.440 --> 05:58.440
So it is valuable to spend the time to get familiar with it,

05:58.440 --> 06:02.240
try it out, and start figuring out where it lands in your comfort zone.

06:02.240 --> 06:03.600
But as you can see here,

06:03.600 --> 06:06.280
there's something really nice about being able to say,

06:06.280 --> 06:10.760
oh, okay, yeah, I'm getting what the user list from where this use user store,

06:10.760 --> 06:12.560
and then you just know where everything is,

06:12.560 --> 06:13.680
everything's named correctly,

06:13.680 --> 06:15.920
or if you need to update it because there's some name clashes,

06:15.920 --> 06:18.360
everything is very declarative and explicit.

06:18.360 --> 06:23.600
So yeah, so these are composables in a nutshell,

06:23.600 --> 06:29.040
and that puts a wrap on the composition API section of all the things we're talking about.

